terministic implementation\n# torch.use_deterministic_algorithms(True, warn_only=True)\n\nprint(\"Random seed:\", SEED)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T11:36:39.149053Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T11:36:39.149346Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.613657Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T11:36:39.149301Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.612907Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Random seed: 42\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import torch\n\n# Check CUDA\nprint(\"PyTorch version:\", torch.__version__)\nprint(\"CUDA available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\n\nDEVICE = 0 if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n\nprint(\"Using device:\", DEVICE)\n\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    print(\"GPU:\", torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.615226Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.615605Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.664231Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.61558Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-20T11:36:44.66368Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"PyTorch version: 2.10.0+cu128\nCUDA available: True\nUsing device: 0\nGPU: Tesla T4\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Goal\n\nThe overall goal is:\n\n> **Given an image containing people, detect individual human heads and estimate the total number of people by counting the detected heads.**\n\n![Detection Goal](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/spl_03/main/images/detection_goal.png)\n\nSuppose an image contains:\n\n```text\nActual people = 120\n```\n\nThe model should detect the individual heads:\n\n```text\n              ┌─────────┐\n              │  head   │\n              └─────────┘\n\n       ┌─────────┐\n       │  head   │\n       └─────────┘\n\n                    ┌─────────┐\n                    │  head   │\n                    └─────────┘\n\n              ...\n```\n\nIf the detector identifies 118 heads, then:\n\n```text\nPredicted heads = 118\n```\n\nand the estimated crowd count becomes:\n\n```text\nPredicted count = 118\n```\n\nSo instead of directly predicting:\n\n```text\nimage → 120\n```\n\nthe model performs:\n\n```text\nimage → head detections → head count\n```\n\nThis is the fundamental idea behind the **detection based crowd counting approach**.\n\n## Head Detection\n\nIn a crowded image, each person is represented by a visible head region.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n┌─────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                  │\n│      ◯        ◯                │\n│                                  │\n│  ◯        ◯       ◯           │\n│                                  │\n│       ◯       ◯        ◯      │\n│                                  │\n└─────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nInstead of generating a continuous density map, the detection-based approach attempts to locate each head individually.\n\nThe model predicts a bounding box around each detected head:\n\n```text\nHead location\n      ↓\nBounding box\n      ↓\nConfidence score\n      ↓\nDetected head\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n                Bounding Box\n          ┌───────────────────┐\n          │        ◯          │\n          │      head         │\n          └───────────────────┘\n                 0.91\n```\n\nHere:\n\n```text\n0.91\n```\n\nrepresents the model's confidence in the detection.\n\n### Bounding Box Annotation\n\nEach annotated head is represented using a bounding box.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\n                x\n                ↓\n          ┌───────────┐\n          │           │\n          │   head    │\n          │           │\n          └───────────┘\n                    ↑\n                    y\n```\n\nA YOLO annotation contains information describing:\n\n```text\nclass\nx-center\ny-center\nwidth\nheight\n```\n\nThe coordinates are normalized relative to the image dimensions.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n0 0.52 0.31 0.06 0.08\n```\n\ncan be interpreted as:\n\n```text\nclass       = head\nx-center    = 0.52\ny-center    = 0.31\nwidth       = 0.06\nheight      = 0.08\n```\n\nThe first value:\n\n```text\n0\n```\n\nidentifies the object as a **head**, because the dataset contains only one class.\n\n### YOLOv8m Head Detector\n\nThe detection model used in this branch is **YOLOv8m**.\n\nThe overall process is:\n\n```text\nInput Image\n     │\n     ▼\nYOLOv8m\n     │\n     ▼\nFeature Extraction\n     │\n     ▼\nHead Detection\n     │\n     ▼\nBounding Boxes + Confidence\n```\n\nThe model is initialized from pretrained YOLOv8m weights and then fine tuned on the SCUT HEAD dataset.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nPretrained YOLOv8m\n        │\n        ▼\n SCUT HEAD Training\n        │\n        ▼\nFine tuned Head Detector\n```\n\nThe model is trained specifically for the single class:\n\n```text\nhead\n```\n\n## High Resolution Detection\n\nThe detector is trained using an image size of:\n\n```text\n1280 × 1280\n```\n\nThis is particularly relevant for crowd images because heads can occupy relatively small regions of an image.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nLow resolution\n\n   ●  ● ●\n ● ● ● ● ●\n```\n\nmay lose some small object information.\n\nUsing a larger input resolution provides the detector with more spatial information:\n\n```text\nHigh resolution\n\n      ┌───┐       ┌───┐\n      │ ● │       │ ● │\n      └───┘       └───┘\n\n  ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐ ┌───┐\n  │ ● │ │ ● │ │ ● │ │ ● │\n  └───┘ └───┘ └───┘ └───┘\n```\n\nThis is useful when detecting heads in distant rows or densely populated scenes.\n\n### Detection Process\n\nConsider an image containing several people:\n\n```text\n┌───────────────────────────────────┐\n│                                   │\n│      ●       ●       ●            │\n│                                   │\n│   ●       ●       ●       ●      │\n│                                   │\n│ ●    ●       ●       ●       ●   │\n│                                   │\n└───────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nYOLO processes the image and produces candidate detections:\n\n```text\n                Input Image\n                     │\n                     ▼\n                  YOLOv8m\n                     │\n                     ▼\n            Candidate Detections\n                     │\n                     ▼\n              Confidence Filter\n                     │\n                     ▼\n             Non Maximum Suppression\n                     │\n                     ▼\n              Final Head Detections\n```\n\nEach final detection contains information such as:\n\n```text\n┌────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ Bounding Box                       │\n│ Confidence                         │\n│ Class = head                       │\n└────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### Confidence Threshold\n\nNot every prediction generated by the detector should be considered a valid head.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDetection 1 → 0.92\nDetection 2 → 0.87\nDetection 3 → 0.63\nDetection 4 → 0.21\nDetection 5 → 0.08\n```\n\nWith a confidence threshold of:\n\n```text\n0.25\n```\n\ndetections below the threshold are discarded.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nYOLO Predictions\n       │\n       ├── confidence < 0.25 → discard\n       │\n       └── confidence ≥ 0.25 → keep\n```\n\nThe remaining detections are then used for counting.\n\n### Non Maximum Suppression\n\nSometimes the detector can produce multiple overlapping predictions for the same head.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n        ┌───────────────┐\n        │   ┌───────┐   │\n        │   │ head  │   │\n        │   └───────┘   │\n        └───────────────┘\n```\n\nThese overlapping predictions may correspond to the same person.\n\n**Non Maximum Suppression (NMS)** removes redundant detections and keeps the most appropriate prediction.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nMultiple predictions\n        ↓\n     NMS\n        ↓\nOne final detection\n```\n\nThis prevents the same head from being counted multiple times.\n\n### From Detection to Count\n\nAfter filtering and NMS, suppose YOLO produces:\n\n```text\nDetection 1\nDetection 2\nDetection 3\n...\nDetection 120\n```\n\nThe number of remaining detections is used as the estimated crowd count:\n\n$$\nC_{YOLO}=N_{\\text{detections}}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n```text\nNumber of detected heads\n            ↓\n       Crowd count\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nDetected heads = 120\n\n        ↓\n\nYOLO Count = 120\n```\n\nUnlike density based counting, there is no need to sum a density map.\n\n### Sparse Crowd Counting\n\nThis detector is particularly useful for the **sparse scene branch**.\n\nThe complete branch can be represented as:\n\n```text\nInput Image\n     │\n     ▼\nYOLOv8m Head Detector\n     │\n     ▼\nHead Bounding Boxes\n     │\n     ▼\nConfidence Filtering\n     │\n     ▼\nNon Maximum Suppression\n     │\n     ▼\nFinal Head Detections\n     │\n     ▼\nCount Detections\n     │\n     ▼\nSparse Crowd Count\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nInput image\n     │\n     ▼\n8 detected heads\n     │\n     ▼\nSparse count = 8\n```\n\nThis approach is straightforward when individual heads can be localized reliably.\n\n## Detection Based vs Density Based Counting\n\nThe main difference between the two approaches can be summarized as:\n\n### Detection based approach\n\n```text\nImage\n  ↓\nDetect individual heads\n  ↓\nBounding boxes\n  ↓\nCount boxes\n  ↓\nCrowd count\n```\n\nFor detection based counting:\n\n$$\nC = \\sum_{i=1}^{N}1=N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of final detected heads.\n\n### Density based approach\n\n```text\nImage\n  ↓\nGenerate density map\n  ↓\nSum density values\n  ↓\nCrowd count\n```\n\nFor density based counting:\n\n$$\nC=\\sum_{x,y}D(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $D(x,y)$ represents the predicted density at each spatial location.\n\nThus, the two branches estimate the same final quantity **the number of people** but use fundamentally different representations.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"!pip install ultralytics -q","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T14:45:36.66167Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T14:45:36.662049Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T14:45:42.814954Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T14:45:36.662023Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T14:45:42.814244Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"\u001b[2K     \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m46.7/46.7 kB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m1.4 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m1.5/1.5 MB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m16.4 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0ma \u001b[36m0:00:01\u001b[0m\n\u001b[2K   \u001b[90m━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\u001b[0m \u001b[32m88.0/88.0 kB\u001b[0m \u001b[31m6.5 MB/s\u001b[0m eta \u001b[36m0:00:00\u001b[0m\n\u001b[?25h","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":3},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import ultralytics\n\nprint(\"Ultralytics (YOLO) version:\", ultralytics.__version__)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T14:45:42.816623Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T14:45:42.816905Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T14:45:47.208916Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T14:45:42.816878Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T14:45:47.208353Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Creating new Ultralytics Settings v0.0.8 file ✅ \nView Ultralytics Settings with 'yolo settings' or at '/root/.config/Ultralytics/settings.json'\nUpdate Settings with 'yolo settings key=value', i.e. 'yolo settings runs_dir=path/to/dir'. For help see https://docs.ultralytics.com/usage/settings.\nUltralytics (YOLO) version: 8.4.157\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Overall workflow\n\nThe complete process is:\n\n```text\nSCUT HEAD Dataset\n       │\n       ├── Pa