 = total number of head bounding boxes\n\nThis is useful because the annotation count approximates the total number of labeled heads in the dataset.\n\n## Combining Part A and Part B\n\nThe next stage creates a single dataset:\n\n```python\nCOMBINED_PATH = Path(\"/kaggle/working/scut_head\")\n```\n\nInstead of training from:\n\n```text\nPart A + Part B\n```\n\nseparately, you create:\n\n```text\nscut_head/\n├── train/\n├── valid/\n└── test/\n```\n\nwith both parts merged into each split.\n\n### Why you rename the files\n\nThis is a very important part of your code.\n\nSuppose Part A and Part B both contain:\n\n```text\n001.jpg\n001.jpg\n```\n\nIf you copied both into the same directory without renaming them, one could overwrite the other.\n\nYou therefore create:\n\n```python\nnew_name = f\"part{part_name}_{f.name}\"\n```\n\nSo:\n\n```text\nPart A:\n001.jpg\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```text\npartA_001.jpg\n```\n\nand:\n\n```text\nPart B:\n001.jpg\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```text\npartB_001.jpg\n```\n\nThis prevents filename collisions.\n\n## Building the copy list\n\nYou iterate through:\n\n```python\nfor part_name, part in [(\"A\", PART_A), (\"B\", PART_B)]:\n```\n\nthen:\n\n```python\nfor split in [\"train\", \"valid\", \"test\"]:\n```\n\nthen:\n\n```python\nfor folder in [\"images\", \"labels\"]:\n```\n\nThis effectively visits:\n\n```text\nPartA/train/images\nPartA/train/labels\nPartA/valid/images\nPartA/valid/labels\nPartA/test/images\nPartA/test/labels\n\nPartB/train/images\nPartB/train/labels\nPartB/valid/images\nPartB/valid/labels\nPartB/test/images\nPartB/test/labels\n```\n\nEach file is added to:\n\n```python\nfiles_to_copy\n```\n\nas a source/destination pair:\n\n```python\n(src, dst)\n```\n\n## Copying the dataset\n\nYou use:\n\n```python\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\n```\n\nto provide a progress bar.\n\nThen:\n\n```python\nshutil.copy2(src, dst)\n```\n\ncopies each file.\n\n`copy2()` is useful because it attempts to preserve file metadata in addition to copying the file contents.\n\nThe resulting structure is:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head/\n\n├── train/\n│   ├── images/\n│   │   ├── partA_...\n│   │   ├── partA_...\n│   │   ├── partB_...\n│   │   └── ...\n│   │\n│   └── labels/\n│       ├── partA_...\n│       ├── partB_...\n│       └── ...\n│\n├── valid/\n│   ├── images/\n│   └── labels/\n│\n└── test/\n    ├── images/\n    └── labels/\n```","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import yaml\n\ndataset_yaml = {\n    \"path\": str(COMBINED_PATH),\n    \"train\": \"train/images\",\n    \"val\": \"valid/images\",\n    \"test\": \"test/images\",\n    \"nc\": 1,\n    \"names\": [\"head\"]\n}\n\nyaml_path = Path(\"/kaggle/working/scut_head.yaml\")\n\nwith open(yaml_path, \"w\") as f:\n    yaml.dump(dataset_yaml, f, sort_keys=False)\n\nprint(yaml_path)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-21T14:48:10.641776Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-09-21T14:48:10.642148Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-09-21T14:48:10.648054Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-09-21T14:48:10.642121Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-09-21T14:48:10.647413Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"/kaggle/working/scut_head.yaml\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# YOLO\n\n![YOLO Architecture](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/masum184e/spl_03/main/images/sparse/yolo/yolo_scut_head.png)\n\n## Creating the YOLO dataset YAML\n\nYou then create:\n\n```python\ndataset_yaml = {\n    \"path\": str(COMBINED_PATH),\n    \"train\": \"train/images\",\n    \"val\": \"valid/images\",\n    \"test\": \"test/images\",\n    \"nc\": 1,\n    \"names\": [\"head\"]\n}\n```\n\nThis is the configuration that tells Ultralytics how to interpret your dataset.\n\nThis file becomes the interface between your dataset and YOLO.\n\n### `path`\n\n```python\n\"path\": str(COMBINED_PATH)\n```\n\nThe root dataset directory:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head\n```\n\n### `train`\n\n```python\n\"train\": \"train/images\"\n```\n\nTraining images are located at:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head/train/images\n```\n\n### `val`\n\n```python\n\"val\": \"valid/images\"\n```\n\nValidation images are located at:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head/valid/images\n```\n\n### `test`\n\n```python\n\"test\": \"test/images\"\n```\n\nTest images are located at:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head/test/images\n```\n\n### `nc`\n\n```python\n\"nc\": 1\n```\n\nmeans there is exactly one object class.\n\n### `names`\n\n```python\n\"names\": [\"head\"]\n```\n\nmeans:\n\n```text\nClass 0 = head\n```\n\nTherefore a YOLO label line beginning with:\n\n```text\n0\n```\n\nrepresents a head.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from ultralytics import YOLO\n\n# Load YOLOv8 medium model for better small object detection\nyolo_custom = YOLO('yolov8m.pt', task='detect')\n\n# Configuration - HEAVILY OPTIMIZED FOR MAXIMUM DETECTION\nCONFIG = {\n    \"batch_size\": 8,  # Reduced for larger image size\n    \"epochs\": 50,  # More epochs for better custom model training\n    \"img_size\": 1280,  # Large size for detecting distant persons\n    \"confidence_threshold\": 0.25,  # VERY LOW - detects even faint persons in rear rows\n    \"iou_threshold\": 0.4,  # Slightly lower for better NMS\n    \"max_det\": 500,  # Very high for crowded classrooms\n    \"seed\": 42,\n    \"patience\": 10\n}\n\n\n# Train on dataset\nprint(\"Starting training...\")\n\nresults = yolo_custom.train(\n        data=str(yaml_path),\n        epochs=CONFIG['epochs'],\n        imgsz=CONFIG['img_size'],\n        batch=CONFIG['batch_size'],\n        device=DEVICE,\n        patience=CONFIG['patience'],\n        save=True,\n        verbose=True,\n        project=\"/kaggle/working/custom_yolo\",\n        name=\"head_detection\",\n        exist_ok=False,\n        seed=CONFIG['seed'],\n        deterministic=True,\n        # amp=False,\n        # workers=0\n)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-09-20T