n-3.12.13 torch-2.10.0+cu128 CUDA:0 (Tesla T4, 14912MiB)\nModel summary (fused): 92 layers, 25,840,339 parameters, 0 gradients, 78.7 GFLOPs\n\u001b[K                 Class     Images  Instances      Box(P          R      mAP50  mAP50-95): 100% ━━━━━━━━━━━━ 55/55 1.8it/s 31.3s0.7ss\n                   all        866      22742       0.93      0.928      0.959      0.498\nSpeed: 0.7ms preprocess, 29.3ms inference, 0.0ms loss, 2.3ms postprocess per image\nResults saved to \u001b[1m/kaggle/working/custom_yolo/head_detection\u001b[0m\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Loading YOLOv8m\n\nYou then import:\n\n```python\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\n```\n\nand load:\n\n```python\nyolo_custom = YOLO('yolov8m.pt', task='detect')\n```\n\nThe model is:\n\n**YOLOv8 Medium — YOLOv8m**\n\nYou are using a pretrained model rather than training the detector from scratch.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nCOCO-pretrained YOLOv8m\n          │\n          ▼\nSCUT-HEAD fine-tuning\n          │\n          ▼\nHead detector\n```\n\nThe model starts with general visual features learned from a large object-detection dataset and adapts them to your head-detection task.\n\n### Why YOLOv8m?\n\nFor crowd scenes, heads can be relatively small, particularly for people far from the camera.\n\nCompared with a smaller model such as YOLOv8n, YOLOv8m has substantially more capacity.\n\nThe trade-off is:\n\n```text\nYOLOv8n\n  ↓\nfaster / lighter\n  ↓\nless computational cost\n\nYOLOv8m\n  ↓\nlarger / more computationally expensive\n  ↓\ngreater model capacity\n```\n\n## Training configuration\n\nYou define:\n\n```python\nCONFIG = {\n    \"batch_size\": 8,\n    \"epochs\": 50,\n    \"img_size\": 1280,\n    \"confidence_threshold\": 0.25,\n    \"iou_threshold\": 0.4,\n    \"max_det\": 500,\n    \"seed\": 42\n}\n```\n\nLet's break these down.\n\n### Batch size\n\n```python\n\"batch_size\": 8\n```\n\nEight images are processed together during each training iteration.\n\nA larger image size consumes considerable GPU memory, so batch size 8 is a reasonable memory-conscious choice.\n\n### Number of epochs\n\n```python\n\"epochs\": 50\n```\n\nThe training dataset is potentially seen up to 50 times.\n\nOne epoch means approximately:\n\n```text\nentire training dataset → model\n```\n\n50 epochs gives the model many opportunities to learn the SCUT-HEAD-specific features.\n\n### Image size\n\n```python\n\"img_size\": 1280\n```\n\nYou train at:\n\n```text\n1280 × 1280\n```\n\nrather than a smaller resolution such as:\n\n```text\n640 × 640\n```\n\nThis is particularly relevant to your application.\n\nIn crowd scenes, distant heads may occupy only a small number of pixels. Increasing the input resolution can preserve more spatial information about small objects.\n\nThe trade-off is significantly higher:\n\n* GPU memory usage\n* training time\n* inference cost\n\n### Confidence threshold\n\nYou define:\n\n```python\n\"confidence_threshold\": 0.25\n```\n\nThis is a relatively low detection threshold.\n\nDuring inference/evaluation, predictions with confidence below the selected threshold are discarded.\n\nConceptually:\n\n```text\nPrediction confidence\n       │\n       ├── 0.05 → reject\n       ├── 0.10 → reject\n       ├── 0.14 → reject\n       └── 0.25+ → keep\n```\n\nA lower threshold can increase recall because more candidate heads are retained.\n\nHowever, it can also increase false positives.\n\nAn important point: this threshold is used during your later `model.val()` call because you explicitly pass:\n\n```python\nconf=0.25\n```\n\nIt is **not a training hyperparameter** in the same sense as the learning rate.\n\n### IoU threshold\n\nYou use:\n\n```python\n\"iou_threshold\": 0.4\n```\n\nIoU means:\n\n**Intersection over Union**\n\nIt measures overlap between two bounding boxes:\n\n$$\nIoU =\n\\frac{\\text{Area of Intersection}}\n{\\text{Area of Union}}\n$$\n\nIoU is used by non-maximum suppression and evaluation-related procedures.\n\nYour relatively lower threshold affects how overlapping detections are handled.\n\n### Maximum detections\n\nYou use:\n\n```python\n\"max_det\": 500\n```\n\nThis allows up to 500 detections for an image during validation.\n\nThis matters for crowd counting because an image can contain a large number of heads.\n\nA low maximum detection limit could artificially cap the possible count.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nActual heads = 350\nmax_det = 100\n```\n\nwould make it impossible for the detector to output more than 100 detections under that configuration.\n\nWith:\n\n```text\nmax_det = 500\n```\n\nthe cap is much less restrictive for the scenes you're targeting.\n\n### Random seed\n\n```python\n\"seed\": 42\n```\n\nA fixed seed improves reproducibility by controlling some random operations.\n\nYou also use:\n\n```python\ndeterministic=True\n```\n\nwhich further emphasizes reproducible behavior.\n\nIt is important to note that complete reproducibility can still depend on the hardware, CUDA/PyTorch versions, and other environmental factors.\n\n## Training the detector\n\nThe actual training starts here:\n\n```python\nresults = yolo_custom.train(\n    data=str(yaml_path),\n    epochs=CONFIG['epochs'],\n    imgsz=CONFIG['img_size'],\n    batch=CONFIG['batch_size'],\n    device=DEVICE,\n    patience=10,\n    save=True,\n    verbose=True,\n    project=\"/kaggle/working/custom_yolo\",\n    name=\"head_detection\",\n    exist_ok=False,\n    seed=CONFIG['seed'],\n    deterministic=True,\n    amp=False,\n    workers=0\n)\n```\n### Dataset argument\n\n```python\ndata=str(yaml_path)\n```\n\ntells YOLO to use:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/scut_head.yaml\n```\n\nThe YAML tells YOLO where the training, validation, and testing images are located.\n\n### Early stopping\n\nYou use:\n\n```python\npatience=10\n```\n\nThis enables early stopping.\n\nIf the monitored validation performance does not improve for 10 epochs, training can stop before reaching all 50 epochs.\n\nSo:\n\n```text\nmaximum = 50 epochs\n```\n\nbut the actual training could finish earlier.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nEpoch 1\nEpoch 2\n...\nEpoch 34\n...\nNo improvement\n...\nEpoch 44\n→ stop\n```\n\nThis helps reduce unnecessary training once validation performance has stopped improving.\n\n### Saving the model\n\n```python\nsave=True\n```\n\nallows training artifacts and model checkpoints to be saved.\n\nYour training output is directed to:\n\n```python\nproject=\"/kaggle/working/custom_yolo\"\nname=\"head_detection\"\n```\n\nTherefore the main directory is:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/custom_yolo/head_detection/\n```\n\nand the trained weights are expected under:\n\n```text\n/kaggle/working/custom_yolo/head_detection/weights/\n```\n\nincluding:\n\n```text\nbest.pt\nlast.pt\n```\n\nTypically:\n\n* `best.pt` = checkpoint corresponding to the best validation performance\n* `last.pt` = checkpoint from the final training epoch\n\n### AMP disabled\n\nYou use:\n\n```python\namp=False\n```\n\nAMP means **Automatic Mixed Precision**.\n\nWith AMP enabled, suitable operations can use lower-precision arithmetic to reduce memory usage and potentially accelerate training.\n\nYou explicitly disable it.\n\nThis can be useful if you want more conservative numerical behavior or if mixed precision causes issues, but it may increase memory consumption and training time.\n\n### Workers = 0\n\n```python\nworkers=0\n```\n\nmeans data loading uses no additional worker processes.\n\nThis is sometimes useful in notebook environments, especially when multiprocessing creates instability.\n\nThe downside is potentially slower data loading.\n\n### Training output\n\nThe training process generates files such as:\n\n```text\ncustom_yolo/\n└── head_detection/\n    ├── weights/\n    │   ├── best.pt\n    │   └── last.pt\n    ├── results.csv\n    ├── results.png\n    ├── confusion_matrix.png\n    ├── confusion_matrix_normalized.png\n    ├── BoxPR_curve.png\n    ├── BoxF1_curve.png\n    ├── BoxP_curve.png\