ion, however, the interpretation differs somewhat from a simple classification confusion matrix because matching predictions to ground-truth boxes involves IoU.\n\n### Normalized confusion matrix\n\n```text\nconfusion_matrix_normalized.png\n```\n\nnormalizes the corresponding values, making relative proportions easier to interpret when counts differ significantly.\n\n### Precision–Recall curve\n\n```text\nBoxPR_curve.png\n```\n\nshows the relationship between:\n\n**Precision**\n\nand\n\n**Recall**\n\nas the confidence threshold changes.\n\nPrecision:\n\n$$\nPrecision =\n\\frac{TP}{TP+FP}\n$$\n\nRecall:\n\n$$\nRecall =\n\\frac{TP}{TP+FN}\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n* TP = true positives\n* FP = false positives\n* FN = false negatives\n\nFor head detection:\n\n#### High precision\n\nMeans relatively few predicted heads are false positives.\n\n#### High recall\n\nMeans the detector finds a large proportion of the actual heads.\n\nFor crowd counting, recall is particularly relevant because missed heads directly contribute to under-counting.\n\n### F1 curve\n\n```text\nBoxF1_curve.png\n```\n\nshows F1 score across confidence thresholds.\n\nF1 is:\n\n$$\nF1 =\n2\\frac{Precision \\times Recall}\n{Precision + Recall}\n$$\n\nIt balances precision and recall.\n\n### Precision curve\n\n```text\nBoxP_curve.png\n```\n\nshows precision as the confidence threshold changes.\n\n### Recall curve\n\n```text\nBoxR_curve.png\n```\n\nshows recall as the confidence threshold changes.\n\nThis can help you understand how sensitive your detector is to the selected confidence threshold.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"model = YOLO(\n    \"/kaggle/working/custom_yolo/head_detection/weights/best.pt\"\n)\n\nmetrics = model.val(\n    data=yaml_path,\n    imgsz=CONFIG[\"img_size\"],\n    batch=CONFIG[\"batch_size\"],\n    conf=CONFIG[\"confidence_threshold\"],\n    iou=CONFIG[\"iou_threshold\"],\n    max_det=CONFIG[\"max_det\"],\n    device=DEVICE\n)\n\nprint(f\"Precision : {metrics.box.mp:.4f}